WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.493
CRA enforcement gap.

00:00:02.443 --> 00:00:05.056
Auditing the poor, ignoring the rich.

00:00:05.056 --> 00:00:11.988
The Canada Revenue Agency audits disability tax credit claims more aggressively than offshore corporate tax avoidance.

00:00:11.988 --> 00:00:18.625
The Auditor General found billions in uncollected corporate taxes from aggressive tax planning and offshore structures.

00:00:18.625 --> 00:00:27.647
The Panama Papers and Paradise Papers revealed Canadian names linked to offshore tax avoidance — CRA's enforcement response was minimal compared to other countries.

00:00:27.647 --> 00:00:34.818
Meanwhile, CRA aggressively audits CERB recipients, disability benefit claimants, and small business owners.

00:00:34.818 --> 00:00:44.352
The enforcement gap is documented: CRA targets those who can't afford tax lawyers while letting those who can afford complex offshore structures pay a fraction of their obligation.

00:00:44.352 --> 00:00:50.613
Comparison table: how CRA treats individuals versus corporations and high-net-worth taxpayers.

00:00:50.613 --> 00:00:53.374
CRA enforcement comparison.

00:00:53.374 --> 00:00:58.352
Disability tax credit claimants: aggressively audited, high rejection rates.

00:00:58.352 --> 00:01:02.659
CERB recipients: mass audits, repayment demands.

00:01:02.659 --> 00:01:06.022
Small businesses: frequent compliance audits.

00:01:06.022 --> 00:01:10.306
Offshore corporate structures: billions uncollected per AG.

00:01:10.306 --> 00:01:13.704
Panama Papers names: minimal prosecutions.

00:01:13.704 --> 00:01:18.284
Large corporations: negotiated settlements, voluntary disclosure.

00:01:18.284 --> 00:01:21.147
Three documented enforcement failures.

00:01:21.147 --> 00:01:24.602
The fiscal enforcement layer of institutional capture.

00:01:24.602 --> 00:01:28.988
CRA enforcement is the fiscal equivalent of regulatory capture.

00:01:28.988 --> 00:01:43.261
The agency that should ensure everyone pays their fair share instead targets those who lack the resources to fight back — disability claimants, CERB recipients, small businesses — while letting those with tax lawyers and offshore structures pay a fraction of their obligation.

00:01:43.261 --> 00:01:47.147
The tax policy gives corporations a fifteen percent rate.

00:01:47.147 --> 00:01:53.272
CRA enforcement ensures even that fifteen percent is negotiable for those who can afford to negotiate.

00:01:53.272 --> 00:02:03.011
The result: the fiscal burden documented across this site falls on wage earners while corporations and high-net-worth individuals benefit from both the policy and the enforcement gap.
